    {  
      "text": "It would allow the island to become more self-sufficient by cutting off all mainland connections.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text shows the island is already self-sufficient, but the debate centers on *improving* connections, not cutting them off. This option misrepresents the purpose of upgrading the ferry."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It would guarantee that property prices would remain low and prevent wealthy developers from moving in.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text suggests a bridge might raise property prices, but says nothing about ferries affecting prices. This is an unsupported assumption that ignores the text’s focus on environmental protection."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It would eliminate the need for any future decisions about infrastructure, as it is a permanent solution.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text explicitly states that upgrading the ferry is uncertain and depends on negotiations and funding, meaning it is not a guaranteed or permanent fix."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It would strengthen the island’s cultural identity by making it more isolated from mainland influences.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text argues that a bridge could weaken identity by making the island feel less unique, but upgrading the ferry is presented as a way to balance access and preservation—not isolation."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It would attract more tourists by offering a scenic, traditional travel experience compared to a modern bridge.",  
      "why_wrong": "While the ferry might be seen as traditional, the text does not claim it would attract more tourists specifically because of its scenic or nostalgic value. This is an unsupported inference."  
    }  
  ]  
